Compute the firmware subpath with relpath, not str.lstrip.

wifi.py builds each firmware file's identity from its path relative to the tree
it was given:

    subpath = dirpath.lstrip(source_path)

str.lstrip takes a SET OF CHARACTERS, not a prefix.  It removes leading
characters of dirpath for as long as each one appears anywhere in source_path,
so it eats into the first directory name whenever that name begins with a
character the source path happens to contain.  The firmware directories are
named by chip and revision -- C-4388__s-C0, C-4364__s-B2 -- so a source path
containing a capital C turns C-4364__s-B2 into -4364__s-B2.  The identity
parser then reads an empty key and dies on `assert not props`, taking the whole
extraction with it.

Whether it happens is decided by the characters in the caller's path, which is
why it looks intermittent: a caller extracting into a temporary directory hits
it whenever mktemp's random name contains one of those letters.

os.path.relpath does what the code means.  It returns "." when dirpath IS
source_path, where lstrip returned "", so that case is normalised to keep the
behaviour identical on the path that already worked.

--- a/asahi_firmware/wifi.py
+++ b/asahi_firmware/wifi.py
@@ -45,7 +45,9 @@
                 dirnames.remove("perf")
             if "assert" in dirnames:
                 dirnames.remove("assert")
-            subpath = dirpath.lstrip(source_path)
+            subpath = os.path.relpath(dirpath, source_path)
+            if subpath == ".":
+                subpath = ""
             for name in sorted(filenames):
                 if not any(name.endswith("." + i) for i in self.EXTMAP):
                     continue
